tant because from a clinical  perspective perspective we really need  to understand how distant cell types are  responding to hypoxia and how this might  affect how they respond to treatment so  today I've presented you two potential  mechanisms which are induced by hypoxia  so for the first looking at the down  regulation of double strand break  recognition and the final one looking a  bit more broad looking at multiple DNA  repair pathways so the next step from  this a really to kind of understand how  are these changes in the DNA repair  proteins regulated by hypoxia what is  directly mechanistically causing these  changes so in the literature when you  look at changes to DNA repair proteins  by hypoxia it's kind of three main  qualities so the first is this direct  reduction of gene expression so  something's action that promoter to  reduce the expression or alternatively  it could be epigenetic changes or even  post translational modifications of the  DNA repair proteins in kind of the  overriding question is is the  hypoxia-inducible factor here involved  in this story and these are the sort of  questions that we hope to answer in the  next few months because if we truly  understand the mechanism behind these  regulations then we can actually start  to manipulate the system and manipulate  the pathways to try and kind of  understand it further to try and improve  treatment methods so I really hope  you've enjoyed gaining an insight into  how we look at hypoxia and brain tumors  in their DNA repair mechanisms thank you  very much for listening I'm more than  happy to take any questions thank you  Thank You Sophie for your informative  presentations we will now start a live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the answer question  box located on the far left of your  screen one of there's many of your  questions as we have time for so let's  get started  our first question is  why is acute hypoxia not sufficient to  induce these changes  so the answer this isn't it very simple  unfortunately and it's very dependent on  which changes we're talking about  so some changes we do see in acute time  points whereas some more at a later time  point and we think this is because that  long-term hypoxia is inducing many  genetic changes such as epigenetic  modifications that require a time period  for those to occur which results in the  changes to the DNA repair mechanisms  that we see so the answer isn't so clear  cut but we have some ideas as to why  that might happen  okay Sophie our next question is how did  you make all of these measurements under  hypoxic conditions or after bringing the  samples out of the one percent o2  environment into room air  so we are actually fortunate enough to  have what's called a hypoxic glove box  so this is an incubator where you can  actually put your hands in it and  culture cells within the actual chamber  so they are maintained within the  hypoxic environment so this means that a  lot of the analysis that we have done  such as the testing the different drugs  and things like that has been done under  the hypoxic environment there is some  experiments where the ROS a period of  reoxygenate however we think this impact  is very small because a lot of the  changes that we see to DNA repair  mechanisms and maintained for a period  of time after oxygenation  so the second part to the question so  I'll assume you did all these  measurements analysis experiments in  room air after bringing the samples out  of the incubator so wouldn't it be more  accurate to describe these results as  being potentially a result of  reoxygenate  as opposed to hypoxia alone so and it's  yeah it's very important to consider the  fact that sometimes we have we  oxygenation however as I said before a  lot of the experiments are done under  permanent hypoxic conditions and however  when we do have real oxygenation we try  and account for that by allowing the  cells - equilibria  so that any oxygen if damage is repaired  for it for example but most of our  experiments are done on there are  hypoxic environments  my next question how are these change  DNA repair genes regulated in hypoxia  so that's kind of the big question that  we're trying to answer at the minute and  there's many documented pathways that  result in DNA repair gene regulation and  hypoxia however it appears to be  different for each different type of  gene each different pathway as well so  these some suggestions that either via  hit a direct mechanism of his-- for  example impacting the promoter at the  genes  however these other mechanisms where you  know it's completely independent and fit  and this is something that we really  want to explore because as soon as we  understand how genes are regulated we  can maybe start thinking about how to  target these mechanisms for therapeutic  benefits  do you think this could have an impact  on the way we treat these tumors in the  clinic that's the ultimate hope and  because so when we think of the clinic  we have to understand that all these  drugs that they using in the clinic so  these tumors are actively damaging the  DNA and if those tumors are hypoxic  something is going to happen to those  DNA repair mechanisms which really might  impact how those tumors are responding  to the treatment so we really do hope  that we're going to you know develop  this further in it'll kind of give us a  better understanding of what's going on  in these tumors and then we can maybe  translate this into the clinic in the  future  so here our next question hypoxia can  affect the cell cycles do you see this  in two results could this have an impact  on your findings  so in the literature is quite well  documented that hypoxia does have an  effect on the cell cycle but what we  found is that this is actually very cell  type-specific so a study from our lab  looks at glioma cells or took lots of  cell lines as well as primary cells from  that have been taken from tumors and we  found that actually hypoxia did not have  an impact on the cell cycle and this is  really important finding because when  you have impacts on cell cycle this can  alter the response to drugs because  often the killing mechanism of drugs  requires cycling cells so our cells  aren't actually impacted as with regard  to the cell cycle and hypoxia which is a  positive thing  and Sophie it looks like we have time  for one more question some drugs don't  work as well in hypoxia so do you know  if you are getting the same amount of  damage in the hypoxic cells as the  coccyx cells  yeah that's a very important question so  what we do is we actually use the comet  assay which I've described in the talk  and to measure the level of damage that  we're achieving in the cells and we  found that with most of our drugs that  we use we do get the same amount of  damage in hypoxic cells as normal Excel  however the some drugs that we don't  work in hypotheses so we have to do  these treatments out of a hypoxic  chamber and we have to consider you know  the impact of the oxygenation within  that experimental setup so yeah we kind  of do look into that and it's a very  important thing to consider  thank you again soapy do you have any  final comments for our audience now just  to say thank you very much for listening  everybody enjoyed the webinar  before we go I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions questions we  did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of the registration we would like  to thank laberd's and our sponsor Thermo  Fisher Scientific for underwriting  today's educational webcast this webcast  can be viewed on-demand through October  of 2018 laberd's will alert you via  email when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that 